      "gap_decomposition_vs_stan": "Not decomposable from data collected here, because no contemporaneous Stan run exists: only Stan's n_eff/s is published, not its per-trial walls or its n_eff. What CAN be said from the Impulse side is where Impulse's own time goes. Per-trial walls remain strongly heterogeneous within a single replicate (rep3: 95.33 / 91.11 / 160.65 / 124.34 s, a 1.76x spread), and the pattern is reproducible across replicates to within about 1 percent per trial position: trial 2 is the slow one in all three runs at 159.64 / 161.06 / 160.65 s. Since all four trials do identical work apart from their RNG seed, that spread is NUTS tree depth, i.e. per-chain trajectory length, not machine noise. The heterogeneity is milder than the baseline's (118 to 226 s, 1.91x) but has the same shape. Closing the remaining 1.7654x would need either shorter trajectories at equal n_eff (better adaptation) or a further cut in per-leapfrog cost; the triangular-recursion rewrite already took the obvious algorithmic slack in the dot products."
